<<~ aka "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/RFC-2119#/normative-language">>



<<~ ahu #/the-seat>>

! The Attestation Plane ~ where the registrar seat hides

''The registrar seat never appears in a spec.'' Five research sweeps across every comparable system found
it three times, and never once in a protocol document:

* in ''Nostr'' it appeared as a '''checkmark''' — NIP-05 states the severability law outright ("clients must
  always follow public keys, not NIP-05 addresses"), and clients drew a badge anyway. The spec held; the
  render policy captured. Nostr was then OFFERED the CA-shaped chain (NIP-85) and REFUSED it.
* in ''Bluesky'' it appeared as an '''AppView allowlist''' — any domain may serve as a handle, and 98.9% took
  the default; `did:web` sat fully specified at six accounts in five million. Then Verification arrived, and
  nine months later a verified `@icegov` became the third most-blocked account on the service.
* in ''Mastodon'' it appeared as a '''login prompt on the server suspending you''' — `Move` must issue FROM
  the old host, signed by the old key, while logged in.

''Therefore the kahu co-signature does not carry the risk. The
CLIENT'S RENDER POLICY and the EXIT PROOF carry it.'' Spec both as tightly as resolution gets spec'd.

''The frame beneath it'' — Zooko's triangle, resolved by Stiegler's petname split. The '''key''' runs global
and unforgeable; the '''nickname''' (the glamour) runs memorable and NON-UNIQUE by design; the '''petname'''
runs local and belongs to the reader. A host-scoped nym asks for the fourth thing the triangle forbids — globally
unique, human-meaningful, AND decentralized — so it MUST pay in an attestor, a price, or a host. The rulings
below decide which, and keep the payment reversible.

''The compressed law from the sweeps:'' a namespace that removes the attestor and keeps the registry (ENS,
Namecoin, Handshake) finds price rate-limits and never adjudicates — ENS settled its own flagship dispute in
a US federal court for ~$1.05M and now petitions ICANN. A namespace that removes the registry and keeps the
attestor (the fediverse) finds judgment works locally and arbitrarily, and no name survives leaving. ''Each
bought what the other lacks; neither bought both.'' The third position — bind the name to a KEY THE HOLDER
KEEPS and attach claims as separate, revocable, logged attestation EDGES — stays unoccupied, and this stack
already stands closest to it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-six>>

!! The Six Rulings (operator, 2026-07-26)

!!! 1 · The severability + render law rides in the CLIENT SPEC

''EDGE, NEVER BADGE.'' A host-scoped nym renders WITH ITS ATTRIBUTOR VISIBLE — `@nym@kowloon.<nexus>` shown ''as
attested by X'' reads as provenance; a bare check reads as a verdict. Spritely names this the ''edge name''
(`Alyssa ⇒ Jane Nym`); a name that appears to come from nowhere has already become a registrar's.

''SEVERABILITY, normative:'' a host-scoped address that stops resolving MUST drop the label and MUST NOT alter
the graph. The durable identity sits BENEATH the name — losing an address costs a convenience, never a self.
Both NIP-05 and AT Proto wrote this law and BOTH survived provider risk because of it.

The dyad renders this natively: a dyad already names vessel × veil, so ''everything renders with its edge
visible'' reads as a property of the ontology rather than a rule bolted onto it.

!!! 2 · Instrument the multi-Nexus FLEET SPAN from day one

''If a decentralization argument depends on users exercising an option, measure the exercise rate or stop
making the argument.'' The most-refuted claim across every sweep: Bluesky opened custom domains to all and
98.9% stayed on the default; Handshake reserved ~80,000 names free for rights-holders and ~7.5% ever claimed.

The measurable here reads: ''what fraction of humans hold dyads across MORE THAN ONE Nexus?'' `fleetSpan`
computes it per fleet. A fleet gathered on one vessel — or inside one Nexus — provides no continuity when
that place dies or turns hostile, so the span IS the exit right, measured.

''No retroactive baseline exists anywhere in the field'' (nobody has re-measured Bluesky since April 2024).
Instrument at founding or lose the baseline permanently.

''OPEN (operator):'' a cross-Nexus rate eventually requires different kahu
groups to SHARE TELEMETRY — which wants its own consent shape, since a naive share would correlate the very
fleets the infra layer keeps un-correlatable.

!!! 3 · The attestor stands SEPARATE from the host

''RULED, and inverted from the obvious arrangement: the kahu HOST; the Nexus's CONTRACTED OPERATORS ATTEST.''
The hand holding the infrastructure power does NOT hold the blessing power.

Why it matters more than hygiene — ''whoever RESOLVES holds final say regardless of who attests.'' OpenNIC
voted 13–2 to drop `.bit`, and a namespace with no attestor at all got adjudicated by the only party who
could still say no. Adjudication relocates; it never vanishes. Keeping the resolving hand and the attesting
hand apart is free today and impossible later.

''THE ACHIEVABLE FORM — SUBSTRATES, NOT HANDS'' (sixth sweep, 2026-08-08). A sweep across ICANN, AT Proto,
Matrix, Nostr, CT, ENS, Tor and OpenNIC found ''no live system'' that separated the attesting hand from the
resolving hand — zero for eight. What every survivor built instead runs one layer down: they made the
resolving hand ''SUBSTITUTABLE'', by seating identity in a key the resolver never mints.
So the ruling's INTENT stands and its MECHANISM restates: ''many resolvers, one
portable key — no single resolver holds final say over IDENTITY, only over REACHABILITY.'' Separation of
substrates buys what separation of hands promised and nobody delivered.

''AND THE PRECEDENT WITHDRAWN.'' ICANN's 1999 registry/registrar split separated ''roles in a market''; it
never split ''custody of a name'' — a registrant stays one legal person under one contract. It reads as no
prior art for quorum custody of a registration, and this meme no longer carries it as one.

''THE FIELD'S OWN NEGATIVE CASE, named:'' Matrix bakes the server domain into the user ID (`@user:example.com`),
so `.well-known` delegation relocates the HOST and never the NAME, and the domain owner holds every identity
on it. Bluesky and Nostr took the other road for the price of one spec page each — the graph stores KEYS,
never handles. ''Any arrangement where a contracted operator's users carry a host-scoped address as IDENTITY
rather than as ADDRESS repeats Matrix exactly'', which is why ruling 1's severability law rides normative.

''The guild vouch law holds here too'' — an attesting operator stakes its own standing, and the lineage
prices concentration among attestors exactly as it prices it among vouchers (`admission-on-a-lineage`).

''THE METER RIDES THE STANDING PRIMITIVES'' (operator-approved, 2026-07-26). Attestation concentration reads
through `vouchConcentration` (one cluster's share of the lineage's whole mass) fed to the convex wall that goes
vertical at the operator's ceiling β — the SAME arithmetic that prices a vouching cartel out as it forms, and
the same refusal to let any authority NAME one. Only a provenance reader wants writing; the mathematics stands.

''PLURALITY NAMES THE PROXY; INDEPENDENCE NAMES THE PROPERTY.'' Ten attestors forming one clique read as one
attestor wearing ten masks — canon says as much in the neighbouring register: "mandatory vouching IS a root
admitter wearing N masks." So the ceiling meters CONCENTRATION rather than counting heads, and it self-tunes as
a Nexus grows where a fixed number never would. What independence measure
survives contact with a real social graph stays genuinely open, and rides research rather than a guess.

!!! 4 · Governance splits from infra duty

''Sovrin died of this, with no adversary at all'' — 10 Stewards (2017) → 60+ (2019) → 1, dissolved 2025-05-21.
Its own post-mortem names the cause: "limited involvement from the Steward community in governance has placed
a heavy burden on the Trustees." ''It bundled governance duty onto unpaid infrastructure operators; the
governance half went unperformed first, and authority pooled in whoever kept showing up.''

A kahu-cabal carrying both roles inherits that failure. ''SPLIT THEM OR FUND THEM.''

''THE MONEY SHORE AND THE ASSET SHORE PART COMPANY'' (sixth sweep, 2026-08-08). Open Collective stands as the
funding-and-accountability shore and MUST NOT become the ''asset'' shore. A fiscal host holds funds ''in its
own bank account'': Open Collective Foundation dissolved in 2024, 600+ collectives lost their host, and only
MONEY could transfer — and only to another 501(c)(3). ''A domain held there becomes the asset of an entity
the kahu do not govern, exposed to that entity's own dissolution.''

What the field shows working for this asset class instead: SPI holds
Debian's trademarks and domains under a member-elected board with documented domain procedures; Software
Freedom Conservancy holds ''domain names'' for member projects à-la-carte, so a project may assign some assets
and retain others. ''Budget the move honestly'' — Mastodon's asset transfer to a nonprofit ran roughly ten
months with a willing founder and zero dispute.

''AND THE SHELL'S OWN FAILURE MODES, kept lit:'' the sponsor that CREATED a project later petitioned to cancel
its trademark (SFLC v. SFC); the `.ORG` registry nearly converted to private-equity ownership and what stopped
it was ICANN — ''the resolving hand, never the nonprofit form''; and a personally-held registration exposes an
insolvency administrator who may pull the domain back into the estate even after transfer.

!!! 5 · An append-only attestation log, with a NAMED OBLIGED WATCHER

Certificate Transparency stands as the strongest positive result in the whole sweep: it ''splits issuance
AUTHORITY from issuance VISIBILITY, then makes visibility a condition of the certificate functioning at
all.'' The CA keeps its job and loses its privacy. Receipts: Symantec 2015 (23 misissued certs became 187),
Ayer's 2017 audit (100+ the CA never self-reported).

So kahu-plane attestations publish to an append-only log the attestor does not control, monitorable by any
Nexus.

''THE WATCHER IS NOT OPTIONAL.'' CT's one real hole — split views — has a specified cure (gossip) with "next
to no deployment in the wild" since 2015. ''A log with no obliged watcher protects nobody.'' Name who must
watch, or the log becomes the failure rather than the fix.

''THE ORDERING RIDES AN EPOCH, NEVER A TIMESTAMP'' (operator correction). CT scopes distrust "by SCT date",
which asserts a global instant — un-pono under no-global-now. The value survives the clock: what the
timestamp bought reads as non-repudiable ORDERING, surgical SCOPING, and third-party WITNESS, and all three
want a ''witnessed partial order'' rather than a shared now. So: ''distrust everything bound at or after
charter-epoch E'' — a content-addressed link in the hash-linked chain, locally verifiable by walking it.
Liveness reads stay LOCAL (`persona-admit`: "no global now; read against a local clock"); ordering rides the
chain. The instrument therefore stands mostly BUILT — `charterChain`, `charterEpochCid`, `boundEpoch`,
persona-KEL `seq`, HandleCard `version`/`prev` already carry it.

''OPEN:'' an attestation issued BETWEEN epochs wants a rule — the chain orders epochs, not the acts inside one.

''THE CADENCE LAW — quorum on the rare act, automation on the frequent one'' (sixth sweep, 2026-08-08). The
sweep found ''no deployed threshold- or quorum-gated certificate issuance anywhere'' — not in specs, not in
CA/Browser Forum ballots, not in products. (Threshold ECDSA for DNSSEC keys and multi-signer DNSSEC both
exist; neither gates ISSUANCE.) The reason reads structural rather than accidental: a sixty-day renewal
cadence cannot wait on humans.

So every survivor ''separates cadences'' — a quorum gates the rare,
slow, high-power act (mint a key, rotate it, admit a member), and automation carries the frequent, fast,
low-power one (issue a leaf). ''A quorum placed in the renewal path gets bypassed inside one outage, and the
bypass becomes permanent.'' The composable approximation that does deploy: pin issuance with CAA `accounturi`
+ `validationmethods` (RFC 8657, mandatory across the CA/B from March 2027); delegate only the
`_acme-challenge` label per operator so proof-of-control splits from zone custody and no operator holds apex
write; and note the shape the industry itself chose when one vantage point could lie — ''a quorum of
OBSERVERS, never a quorum of AUTHORIZERS.''

Name-constraining a subordinate CA bounds the DELEGATE and never the DELEGATOR: no X.509 mechanism removes a
parent's own power to issue.

!!! 6 · The host-scoped nym rides as a LEASE

''APPROVED IN PRINCIPLE ONLY — this rung awaits unpacking and
carries no RULED language yet.''

''Rent gates the flood, never the claim.'' Three price points across four orders of magnitude produced one
outcome: Namecoin at ~free yielded 28 non-squatted names with real content out of 196,023; ENS at $5–640/yr
saw only 22% of names ever set a record; Handshake's burned Vickrey bids produced 11M+ TLDs nobody resolves.
''Never let anyone argue rent decides WHO DESERVES a name.''

What rent measurably DOES buy reads as ''garbage collection'' — 1.17M ENS names expired that nobody wanted,
against 241K contested. That recirculation suits a rival good — the host-scoped address rides as exactly that,
and every other capture-answer in this stack (fork-as-exit, the capture clock) assumes it away.

''ONE HARD RULE ALREADY EARNED:'' expiry MUST kill RESOLUTION in the same act it kills the grant. ENS keeps
resolution live past expiry and 2,633 payments landed with strangers who dropcaught a lapsed name. ''A rental
namespace whose expiry does not invalidate resolution converts forgetfulness into theft.''

''AND THE CAUTION THE LEASE CARRIES:'' the sweeps' cross-cut finding reads — in both failed models the name
bound to a ''rented substrate'' (an on-chain rental contract, a hostname) and never to a key the holder keeps.
So the lease stays right for RECIRCULATION and wrong as IDENTITY. It composes here only while the severability
boundary (#/1) holds absolutely: ''lease the address, never the self.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-stays-open>>

!! What Stays Open

* ''What does a sanction TARGET?'' The dyad model makes the ladder's targets precise for the first time —
  sanctioning a dyad (this human on this device), a veil, a vessel (all its faces), or a fleet run as four
  different acts with four different blast radii. The antigen bans a nym; WHICH one? Ostrom P5 (graduated
  sanctions) reads violated while the board runs binary, and the fediverse measured the cost (of 1,807
  instances only 20.1% publish a blocklist; 46.4% of those state a reason; no appeals machinery anywhere).
* ''What is the unit of COLLECTIVE CHOICE?'' Ostrom P3 reads violated — a 2-of-3 signing quorum makes
  operators rule-takers. Keyed to vessels, a human with five vessels casts five votes. Keyed to the Handle,
  only public faces vote. Keyed to the fleet, membership must prove WITHOUT linking the dyads.

  ''MOSTLY DISSOLVED — the contract form removes the electorate'' (operator, 2026-08-08). The quorum signs a
  ''CONTRACT to one operator'' — bilateral, leased, attenuating, held and presented by its holder — never a
  list of who counts. Verification runs peer-local against the Nexus key, so no node needs the set and no
  writer decides what //current// means. ''A list IS a global now'', and the field's own case makes the cost
  concrete: the one identity layer that stayed single-writer for years against stated intent was the one
  built as a DIRECTORY. Revocation without a list rides ''non-renewal of the lease'' plus the append-only
  witness of #5, which stays monotone — a revocation not yet seen means an island not yet synced, which reads
  honest, where a stale list silently reads as the wrong answer.

  What survives the dissolution: ''who the kahu keyholders are'' (a bounded threshold key, never a roll of the
  governed) — and the standing refusal of ''token or stake weight'', since a namespace root moved onto
  token-weighted governance takes capture-by-capital with it. Weighting by user count would hand every
  operator a Sybil printing press; an operator manufactures users, and cannot manufacture another operator
  willing to bear cost.
* ''The rival good — RULED (operator, 2026-07-26).'' A fork that walks away with vessels ''pays in socially
  managed resources.'' Everything the mesh reaches OUTWARD to — the first two Nexus namespaces
  `*.amorphousdreams.net` and `*.elyncia.net` among them (#/nexus-topology) — rides socially-held custody
  rather than cryptographic custody. A fork leaving those behind ''accepts that cost'' and re-founds
  equivalent capability ''as a new place.''

  ''THE ISOMORPHISM CARRIES IT.'' A fork that leaves the leased resources falls back to a ''local rhizome''
  exactly as a vessel-veil identity falls back to the ''anon floor'' — same law, two planes. Nothing is seized
  and nothing is destroyed; the departing party simply arrives holding what it brought, which reads as the
  honest reading of leaving rather than a penalty imposed on it. The lease chain stands pono: a leased resource
  never becomes a possession, so losing it on the way out costs a capability and never a self.

  So "who holds the zone after a split" ''wants no cryptographic answer and can have none'' — it names a
  registrar account and a legal person, which sits OUTSIDE what this stack governs. The design's part reads:
  make the host-scoped nym structurally cheap to lose (severable · leased · identity surviving it whole), so a fork's
  question shrinks from "who owns the name" to "who stands up a resolver."

  ''CHOOSING WHICH FAILURE TO ACCEPT, never whether to fail'' — and the choosing runs at every scale: kahu,
  operator, user.
* ''Exit may COMFORT rather than discipline.'' Hirschman's lazy monopoly: a volunteer cabal running
  infrastructure it wanted anyway loses little when critics depart, and cheap forking plus portable identity
  produces exactly the mobility asymmetry that lets a core purchase internal peace by shedding its critics.
  Candidate cure, unruled: a clean fork emits a signed public ''reasoned notice of departure'', so exit
  transmits voice instead of draining it.

<<~/ahu>>

